tell us a little bit about Lewis wolf  and his journalistic publications who is  Lewis and what has he been doing over  the years and why is the government so  outraged by him  well his background is it's thoroughly a  legitimate standard if you will  journalism it was a sign for a couple of  years in Manchester writing for a couple  of prominent news organizations and  England yes Manchester England and  eventually made his way through London  back to the United States where he  gravitated to a scrutiny of the CIA and  the intelligence community watching the  revelations of the Church Committee  information that he had come across in  his research some of which he had  published as part of news stories  previously other parts of which he was  not permitted to publish by the nature  of the press establishment and so he put  together with Bill shap and Ellen Rea  the covert action information bulletin  whose function was to focus on the  intelligence organizations of the United  States and discuss and expose in a  perfectly legitimate way the covert  action activities of the CIA  principally as well as the other  intelligence organizations  I believe they formed that in about 1976  and have been in Washington since  publishing stories of true stories their  quality their standards of journalism  are exceedingly high that bulletin is  distributed in the State Department and  it's sold out the first morning that  it's put on the stands in the State  Department newsstands they also publish  in that bulletin the names of CIA agents  and where they are now as they're  assigned and reassigned about the world  I believe that in very recent months  they have ceased the policy of  publishing names because the controversy  is so hot and they were just too soon  and and also it's never been their  intention to break the law they've made  that clear from the outset  bill shap is the brother is a highly  competent attorney and is the brother of  top sportscaster in New York City he is  a very articulate man himself and has  testified to the Senate many times  against the names of agents bill for  example and and defending what he and  Lew will for doing and after his  testimony many times senators have come  up and shaken his hand and said I  disagree with you but you certainly make  your case for the legitimacy of what  you're doing very well  yes let's yeah let's take a look at some  of the things which Lewis wolf had to  say and we'll let you comment ever so  often on glass okay what in the years  that you've been publishing it you think  are some of your most significant  revelations I there's some stories that  even shocked you well I think one of the  big stories that we were able to  document in some detail was the the rent  the whole scope of CIA activities  similar to what the CIA did in Chile in  which was documented by the Senate  Select Committee was the whole covert  action program in Jamaica in between  1976 and 1980 and we were we did a great  deal of research into that and we  discovered many in many ways a very  similar program almost a classic model  that was followed along the lines of  what they did in Chile including three  major categories military power military  activity the use of propaganda in the in  the media and the third one which was  economic warfare using the multilateral  organizations the International Monetary  Fund and others to withhold loans which  had been promised to that you make in  government this is one example what I  cut them were the details of that you  particularly mention those first two  examples of actual paramilitary activity  well CIA was involved in in Jamaica  there was a fire for example that took  place in Jamaica of an old people's home  I think 150 old and infirmed women many  of them blind we're living in this home  and as it turned out the place burned  down in the space of about eight minutes  seven or eight minutes it was discovered  that the phone wires had been cut and  that their exits had all been blocked  even those who could see their way out  could not get out and that the means by  which the fire was set was the use of a  petroleum jelly which was unavailable in  Jamaica this was one indication among  others that there was outside help why  was why would the CIA you might ask why  would the CI want to murder  150 old women it was because it was an  effort part of a very large-scale effort  to discredit the government in Jamaica  which was at the time headed by Michael  Manley the prime minister there were  images produced of Michael Manley in the  media in the Daily Gleaner which was the  newspaper which the United States  through the CIA helped to support and  there is a good deal of evidence of that  there were images of Michael Manley  showing a death mask I mean was his face  which had been somehow made to look like  a death mask although it was pretending  to be a photograph of him and there were  many ways to try and discredit him this  is very very similar in fact to what the  CIA did to try and discredit Salvador  Allende in Chile if you remember Richard  Nixon instructed CIA in a meeting which  was in 1970 in the White House and there  was a document in the Senate report  which is reprinted and he said make the  economy scream those were his words and  that's of course what the CIA proceeded  to do in Chile and that's what they also  did in Jamaica they also had furnished a  lot of munitions and assistance to the  terrorists squads that were going around  people in in those cities and also in  the countryside in Jamaica as well  that's correct  well John you had a trip down to Jamaica  and you saw a lot of these things about  Jamaica with your own knives right yes  indeed very moving a very interesting  trip to me to see a target country at a  time in the height of a CIA operation  when I was completely outside so there's  no way that I could see to see the  problem that journalists have always had  when the US government was targeting on  a country in a situation trying to  destabilize it overthrow or manipulate  the elections or whatever of trying to  figure out what's going on when you  don't have access to all of the flow of  cables and the discussions and National  Security Council meetings and whatnot  for the planning I observed that from  published information a CIA station  there was quite large for that size of a  country it was huge in fact it was  bigger than any station I knew of in  Africa although Jamaica was a fairly  small island with two million people  that's typical of when the CIS is got a  big operation going in a place they  beefed up the station and beefed up the  offices back stateside the tone and flow  of articles in The Gleaner was of course  as Lou says definitely a chapter out of  Chile or a chapter out of the Angolan  operation every indication of a massive  CI operation going to destabilize the  government to make the economy screen  and in that case I would say that  probably someone got promoted for that  one probably several people because  Manley was right because it worked by a  legitimate yeah without without a  scandal without an assassination without  the things that have caused the CI so  much grief a manly a champion of social  democracy of giving the people a piece  of the pie I was thrown out of office  and an arch capitalist  I was put in office and and again there  was a minimal adverse publicity lure  wolf and the others were studying the  situation and did publish about what was  going on but it was not published in the  New York Times in the Washington Post  and the big the big television networks  didn't do Studies on it primarily  because even if the journalists of the  big organs knew what was happening and  might have wanted to they really just  because of the secrecy couldn't get the  truth about what the United States was  doing one interesting thing was that the  effectiveness of the lies the liars in  our government you know the duplicity  when we form the CIA we committed our  leaders to having two policies as loose  as the rhetoric the public statements  and the truth about what the United  States policy is so an interesting  measure of presidents is how effectively  they lie interesting discussions that  occur inside the CIA is about lying  would would you lie you know you're the  d-do and you're going to testify to the  Senate and serious discussions are you  going to lie because if you do it's  perjury and there's a chance you could  go to jail and yet you know the  organization expects you to lie the  President Carter was one of the most  effective Liars I have ever watched on  television and through the media as a  poker player men I would never go up a  man he could look at that camera and say  things that obviously he knew were  completely and totally false and I would  study his expression and I could detect  nothing except occasionally a little  wisp of a smile that wasn't consistent  with what he was saying like he thought  it was funny but one of his more prized  attack actually well the the smile that  I was referring to wasn't that it was  just a kind of like he was repressing a  grin like he thought it was funny  how he rationalized these things I don't  know but one of his most  conspicuous lies was when he sat down  with manly just a few weeks before the  elections in Washington and told him  face to face  that the CI don't have the CI doing  anything to destabilize you or your  government we have no such program going  when obviously he did and he pulled it  off to the point where even Manley was  troubled very effective ly liars

